name: tm-chotu-rx-cadence
description: The prescription (Rx) lifecycle and molecule-dosage cadence model — how an order moves through the doctor portal (digitized → doctor consult → HA consult → confirmed → scheduler), what each status ACTUALLY means versus what its label says, the Rx register (register_details), how to tell a fresh consultation from a system-reused Rx via user_details.doctor_invoice_name, the salt-dosage decode, and the days-of-supply derivation. Load when asked about prescriptions, Rx generation, doctor or Health Advisor consultations, doctor portal statuses, doctor TAT or workload, days of supply, refill timing, run-out dates, dosage or frequency decoding, or anything joining patient-level therapy to orders. NOTE: this skill maps the DATA and the locked derivations — it does not predict due dates; refill prediction and cohort targeting belong to DCOE.
---

# Rx lifecycle + molecule-dosage cadence

Two things live here: **how a prescription order actually moves through the doctor portal**, and **how to compute when a patient runs out of medicine**. Both are full of traps where the obvious reading is wrong.

---

## 🔴 The governing rule: the status label is not the status meaning

Truemeds order statuses were named early and reused later. **Several codes mean different things depending on where they sit in the flow, and two are used for two different actors.** Reading a status by its English name will produce confident, wrong analysis.

## The doctor-portal flow — LOCKED (user-taught 2026-08-19)

```
39  DIGITIZED                  order reaches the DOCTOR PORTAL
  ↓
209 DOCTOR ASSIGNED            ⚠️ also used for HA assignment — see below
  ↓
215 CALL ATTEMPTED             ⚠️ also used for the HA call
  ↓
  ├─ doesn't connect  → 216 ORDER ON HOLD
  └─ connects, consultation completes
        ├─ HA NOT required → straight to 317 (452 never fires)
        └─ HA required ↓
452 PARTIALLY DIGITIZED        🔴 = DOCTOR consult DONE, awaiting HA. ONLY on the HA path
  ↓
209 DOCTOR ASSIGNED (again)    ⚠️ this time it is the HA being assigned
  ↓
215 CALL ATTEMPTED (again)     ⚠️ this time it is the HA call
  ↓
317 DR ORDER CONFIRMED         = ALL doctor-portal consultations complete (doctor AND HA)
  ↓
scheduler picks the order up → moves it to PROCESSING (the real warehouse assignment)
  └─ on failure → 318 SCHEDULER ERROR DR CONFIRM ORDER
```

### What each of these actually means

| Code | Label | What it really means |
|---|---|---|
| **452** | PARTIALLY DIGITIZED | 🔴 **NOT a transcription state.** The doctor consultation is **done** and the order is waiting on the **HA** consultation. **It fires ONLY when an HA consult is required** — if no HA is needed the order reaches `317` without ever stamping `452`. So 452 is effectively the *HA-required* marker, not a doctor-consult marker |
| **209 / 215** | DOCTOR ASSIGNED / CALL ATTEMPTED | 🔴 **Used twice each — once for the doctor leg, once for the HA leg.** There is no separate "HA assigned" code. **Position relative to 452 is what tells you which actor it was**: before 452 = doctor, after 452 = HA |
| **317** | DR ORDER CONFIRMED | **All doctor-portal consultations are complete or were not required** — the scheduler's go-signal. **Not** "a doctor confirmed it" |
| **233** | WAREHOUSE ASSIGNED | 🔴 **Not physical assignment.** It means a warehouse id was attached for **pricing and catalogue**. The real warehouse assignment is the scheduler moving the order to **processing** |
| 318 | SCHEDULER ERROR DR CONFIRM ORDER | The scheduler failed to pick up a 317 order |

### Consequences you must apply

- **Never count doctor calls off `215` alone** — it is inflated by HA calls. Disambiguate by whether the event precedes or follows `452`.
- **🔴 Never count doctor consultations off `452`.** It only fires when an HA consult follows, so it **undercounts by every doctor-only order**. `452` answers "how many orders needed an HA", not "how many doctor consults happened". The 17-Aug ratio (7,397 with 452 against 24,476 reaching 317) is the HA-required share, not a consult rate.
- **Never treat `317` as doctor workload.** Measured 17-Aug: of 24,475 orders carrying a 317, **5,636 (23%) were stamped by a single system actor on the `workflow_status = 242 NO_DOCTOR_CALL` path** — auto-confirm, no consultation, correct by design. Human doctor/HA actors sat at ~82–178 orders each per day.
- **`workflow_status = 242 NO_DOCTOR_CALL`** is the auto-confirm path: a valid Rx already attached ⇒ no consultation needed ⇒ straight to 317 so the scheduler can proceed. Correct behaviour, not a defect.
- **Don't read `233` as "it's in a warehouse".** For physical fulfilment state, use processing, not 233.

### ❌ `595 HEALTH ADVISOR CALL ATTEMPTED` is DEAD CODE — do not use it

It exists in the ORDER STATUS master and is **never stamped**. Probed 17-Aug-2026 over ~62k orders: **zero rows.** The HA leg reuses `209` / `215`. Any funnel or HA-volume metric built on `595` returns zero and looks like "no HA activity" rather than a broken metric.

### Measured corroboration of the flow (17-Aug-2026)

| Code | Orders | Events | **Events per order** |
|---|---|---|---|
| 209 DOCTOR ASSIGNED | 20,243 | 47,757 | **2.36** |
| 215 CALL ATTEMPTED | 20,193 | 55,058 | **2.73** |
| 452 PARTIALLY DIGITIZED | 7,397 | 7,413 | **1.00** |
| 317 DR ORDER CONFIRMED | 24,476 | 24,497 | **1.00** |

`209` and `215` firing ~2.4–2.7× per order is the signature of **two legs** (doctor, then HA); `452` and `317` fire exactly once as one-time checkpoints. Note also that only **7,397** orders hit `452` against **24,476** reaching `317`. **Confirmed:** `452` fires only when an HA consult is required — orders needing no HA go to `317` without it. So that ratio is the **HA-required share**, not a doctor-consult rate. (One-day probe; re-measure before quoting it.)

---

## `register_details` — the Rx register (DB 170 `tmmumpsdb`)

Summary-level prescription register. **This is the source that actually carries doctor identity and a doctor-confirmation timestamp** — `order_status` only carries an actor id you would have to resolve.

| Column | Use |
|---|---|
| `doctor_id`, `prescriber_name` | the **in-house** doctor who processed the order (`prescriber_name` is a saved copy of that doctor's name — never the external prescriber off an uploaded Rx) |
| **`doctor_confirm_date`** | **real doctor-confirmation timestamp** — prefer this over inferring from status 317 |
| `med_change_after_dr_call` (bool) | did the medicine change after the consult |
| `current_order_id`, `previous_order_id` | **the cadence linkage** — this fill vs the prior one |
| `medicine_composition`, `medicine_drug_type`, `medicine_schedule` | molecule, form, and drug schedule (H / H1 / X) |
| `drug_name`, `product_code`, `quantity` | what was dispensed |
| `fsp_id` | joins into the substitution chain (`final_substitute_product.final_subs_id`) |
| `checker_id`, `checker_name`, `batch_number`, `serial_number` | dispensing/audit trail |
| `invoice_number`, `warehouse_id`, `supply_date`, `expiry_date` | fulfilment |
| 🔴 `drx_link`, `invoice_link` | **document URLs — P0 PII. Never export, never paste.** See `tm-chotu-pii-register` |

---

## 🔒 Rx GENERATION — which doctor processed it, and was it a fresh consultation?

**This is the canonical route. Do NOT go to `order_status` for Rx counts or doctor attribution.**

### Grain first

`register_details` is **order × line-item** grain. Always roll up:

```sql
SELECT current_order_id, prescriber_name, doctor_id
FROM tmmumpsdb.register_details
GROUP BY 1,2,3          -- distinct order × doctor, never COUNT(*)
```

`COUNT(*)` counts dispensed medicines, not prescriptions, and inflates by several times.

### Two sources, in order

**SOURCE 1 (first) — `register_details.prescriber_name`.** The clean way to check **which doctor processed the order**. This is where you start. Roll up at distinct `(current_order_id, prescriber_name)`.

🔒 **`prescriber_name` is the IN-HOUSE Truemeds doctor only** — a copy of that doctor's name saved onto the register. It is **not** the external prescriber transcribed off a customer's uploaded prescription. That is precisely why comparing it against `user_details.doctor_invoice_name` is meaningful: both are in-house doctor identities, one a denormalised copy, one the master.

**SOURCE 2 (second) — the user master, reached via the register's `doctor_id`:**

```
register_details.doctor_id  →  user_details.id  →  user_details.doctor_invoice_name
```

**`user_details` is on DB 170 `tmmumpsdb`** (verified 2026-08-19) — same database as `register_details`, so this is a local join, not a cross-DB hop.

### The test: compare source 1 against source 2

| `prescriber_name` vs `doctor_invoice_name` | Meaning |
|---|---|
| **MATCH** | ✅ **FRESH consultation** — a doctor actually consulted on this order |
| **DO NOT match** | 🔴 **No doctor consultation happened on this order.** The system processed it using an Rx generated earlier — and **`previous_order_id` is the order that Rx came from** |

So `previous_order_id` is not merely a cadence link: on a name mismatch it is **the provenance of the reused prescription**. That comparison is how you separate genuine consultation volume from system-carried Rx.

⚠️ A mismatch is **not** a data error. It is the documented signature of Rx reuse. Treat it as a category, not a defect. And if the reused share comes back large, that is **not** a defect finding either — it is the auto-confirm path working as designed. Read it as consult **load**, not consult **failure**.

**Three grain edges to settle before reporting:**
- **Multi-doctor orders** — if one order carries register rows with different `doctor_id`, fresh and reused can both fire and the buckets will not sum to the total. Check grain on the sample and set a tie-break first.
- **Unresolved** — a `doctor_id` that fails to join `user_details` is its own bucket. Never fold it into fresh or reused.
- **The name test is a string compare** — normalise case and whitespace; if a meaningful near-match tail exists, report it separately rather than forcing it onto a side.

### When `order_status` IS the right tool

Only for **ledger-style actor tracking** — who stamped `452 PARTIALLY DIGITIZED`, who stamped `317 DR ORDER CONFIRMED`, call attempts, holds. That is event attribution, not Rx counting. For "how many Rx", "which doctor", "was it fresh", `register_details` + `user_details` is the answer and `order_status` is the wrong table.

### Probe note

Filtering `register_details` on `doctor_confirm_date` **timed out** on a single-day window (2026-08-19) — that column looks unindexed. Scope by `current_order_id` (or an order-id list from a bounded `order_details` pull) rather than a date range on this table.

---

## Days of supply — a documented DERIVATION, not a chotu prediction

### Source tables

| Table | Role |
|---|---|
| **`salt_dosage_prescribed_by_doctor`** (DCOE shorthand: **SDPBD**) | **Canonical Rx line source.** Carries `fsp_id`, `patient_id`, `ordered_quantity`, `available_prescribed_quantity`, `prescribed_dosage`, `frequency`, `prescribed_dosage_unit_quantity`, `prescribed_duration`, `active`, `createdon`. ~26.5M rows.<br>**Lives on MySQL — Main DB (DB 2) or Mangesh DB (DB 630)**, schema `TMMUMPSDB` UPPERCASE. Both are **prod**: extract by indexed key and compute locally, never an analytical GROUP BY there.<br>**Reaching an order:** it carries `fsp_id`, not `order_id` — hop `SDPBD.fsp_id → final_substitute_product.final_subs_id → .order_id` (FSP = `final_substitute_product`). That hop is also how you get to `customer_id` and a delivery-date anchor |
| **`salt_dosage_master_data_mapping`** | **The only decode truth.** 38 rows. `id` **is** the code, and `prescribed_dosage`, `frequency` and `prescribed_duration_unit` **all share one keyspace** — which is why codes run to 22 |
| `salt_dosage_master` | the per-salt template (46,980 active) with `max_allowed_*` ceilings |
| ❌ `dosage_master` | **9-row legacy — STALE, DO NOT USE.** Its decode is wrong (id 4 = '1-0-0' when the real `prescribed_dosage=4` is '1-0-1' = 2/day) and it lacks codes 16–22 |
| ❌ `analytics_salt_dosage_prescribed_by_doctor` (tmprod) | **FROZEN at 2026-02-14**, zero rows after 1 Aug — despite holding 25.5M rows |

### The decode

| `type` | ids | `value` means |
|---|---|---|
| `DOSAGE` | 3–9, 16, 17, 21, 22 | **doses per day** — '1-1-1'→3, '1-0-1'→2, '1-0-0'→1, 'SOS'→NULL |
| `FREQUENCY` | 13–15, 18–28 | **cycle length in days** — Everyday→1, alternate→2, weekly→7, 3-monthly→90 |
| `DURATION_UNIT` | 10, 11, 12 | days multiplier — Day→1, Week→7, Month→30 |
| `ADVICE` | 1, 2, 29–38 | food/timing text, no `value` — irrelevant to DoS |

### 🔒 The formula

```
doses_per_day = DOSAGE.value / FREQUENCY.value                  -- administrations per day
units_per_day = doses_per_day × prescribed_dosage_unit_quantity -- uq DOES multiply
DoS_days      = ordered_quantity / units_per_day
run_out_date  = COALESCE(actual_delivery_date, order_placed_date) + DoS_days
```

- **`ordered_quantity` is already in CONSUMABLE units** (tablets / ML / GM) — **no pack conversion.** Verified four ways, including `ordered_quantity = fsp.pack × fsp.subs_quantity` holding ~77% exactly and the packs-hypothesis holding ~0.4%. `medicine_master.pack` is a data-quality monitor only, never a DoS input. `strength` is potency, never a multiplier.
- **`uq` multiplies** — this was flip-flopped and then settled empirically: on edited-quantity lines (where the template can't vouch for itself) **WITH-uq won 488 to 27 (94.8%)**, and realised reorder behaviour agreed.
- **SOS lines (`prescribed_dosage = 16`) have no deterministic cadence — exclude them** (~1.87%).
- Pre-filter **`SDPBD.active = 1`** (~21% of lines are superseded).
- Computable fraction ≈ **98.1%**.
- Join the 38-row decode master **twice** — once `type='DOSAGE'` on `prescribed_dosage`, once `type='FREQUENCY'` on `frequency`.

### 🚩 The biggest trap: the line is a COPY of the salt's template

Measured on **3,157,301** lines since 2026-07-01:

| line field vs its master | identical |
|---|---|
| `prescribed_dosage` | **98.9%** |
| `frequency` | **97.7%** |
| `prescribed_dosage_unit_quantity` | **97.5%** |
| `prescribed_duration` | **96.5%** |
| `prescribed_quantity` | 81.1% |

➡️ **DoS is a per-SALT standard rate applied to a per-PATIENT quantity — not a doctor's bespoke instruction.** Nothing customer-facing may imply the doctor personally set this schedule.

The only genuinely per-order fields are **`ordered_quantity`** (what was dispensed) and **`available_prescribed_quantity`** — which is **remaining entitlement on the prescription, NOT stock in the patient's cupboard.**

⚠️ **Because `uq` equals the master ceiling on 97.5% of lines, you are usually multiplying by a CEILING, not an actual.** Known master defects: GLIMEPIRIDE reads 4 tablets/day; TELMISARTAN 40 MG carries `1-0-1` (2/day) though it is once-daily. A single wrong template field silently overestimates consumption for **every patient on that salt** and nudges them early — on the worked example it swung the governing run-out date by a **week**.

**Ship a confidence flag:** `HIGH` when `uq` deviates from the master max (someone set it deliberately), `LOW` when it equals the max (inherited ceiling).

#### DCOE prefers the empirical per-salt rate over the template

46,980 masters cannot be hand-fixed, and don't need to be — derive the rate from realised refills:

```
implied_units_per_day = ordered_quantity(fill N) / days_until(fill N+1)   -- median per salt
```

SDPBD `createdon` is set at **order** time, so intervals need no delivery lookup and the order→delivery lag cancels between two fills.

### DCOE's modelling decisions — recorded so you can READ their output, not so chotu reproduces them

🔴 **chotu does not predict refill dates.** It shows where the data lives and what the locked derivations are. "When is this customer due", "who is overdue", "next expected order date" are **DCOE model outputs** — point at DCOE, do not compute them here.

The rules below are recorded because you will need them to interpret a DCOE result, not as instructions for chotu to run.

#### Scoped to primary-disease (D1) salts only

**An OTC or non-primary-disease line must never dictate cadence.** A paracetamol strip bought alongside a chronic refill has its own short, irregular run-out and drags `next_expected_order_date` earlier — manufacturing a "due" customer who is not due.

Gate (already implemented — reuse, do not re-derive):
```
disease_product_mapping WHERE type='DISEASE' AND priority='D1' AND active=1
  → LOWER(dpm.product_code) = LOWER(fsp.product_code)
  → dpm.type_id = disease_master.id → disease_master.disease
```
Non-D1 and unmapped lines are computed but carried as an **EXCLUDED** flag for diagnostics only.

⚠️ **Scope FIRST, then roll up.** Rolling up before gating is the exact bug this rule exists to prevent.

#### Grain is per-patient, not per-customer

**Cadence key = `(customer_id, patient_id, molecule)`.** One account orders for several patients on unrelated intervals — a father's 30-day chronic refill and a child's 5-day antibiotic collapse into a ~17-day phantom cadence matching neither, and the customer gets chased on a date no patient is due.

`patient_id` is on SDPBD already. **The SEND is per customer** (the account is who we message) **but the DUE DATE is per patient**, and the D1 gate applies *within* each patient. Never collapse to customer grain at any layer, including the feature mart — that discards a fact that cannot be re-derived downstream.

**A patient whose D1 lines are all SOS/uncomputable has NO dosage cadence.** That is an honest UNKNOWN — never a defaulted date. Fall back to empirical rhythm and label which predictor produced the date.

#### DCOE's test case

**`order_id = 52637339`** — a real order placed by the owner for his father (`patient_id 6061442`), i.e. a genuine customer ≠ patient row. Validate the DoS formula, the D1 gate and the patient split against it line by line.

---

## 🔴 The boundary — what chotu does and does not do here

**chotu surfaces sources and locked definitions. It does not forecast.**

- ✅ "Days of supply is computed as follows, from these tables, with this caveat."
- ✅ "The Rx register lives here; this column means that; here is how to tell a fresh consultation from a reused Rx."
- ❌ "This customer is due on the 12th." — that is a prediction, and it belongs to DCOE's model.

**Why this line exists, with evidence:** DCOE back-tested refill-date logics on **n = 956,453** held-out (customer, patient, salt) pairs. **Days-of-supply came out worse than predicting a flat 34 days for everyone** — template DoS mdAE 20.2d and early 83% of the time, against 11.0d for flat-34 and **6.4d** for residual-life. DoS correctly answers *"when does the medicine in the cupboard run out"*. It is **not** the answer to *"when should we message them"*, and a plugin that hands someone a due-date implies an accuracy it cannot defend.

If a user asks for a due date, give them the data map and route them to DCOE.

## See also

`tm-chotu-tables-enums` (status decodes) · `tm-chotu-business-flows` (order lifecycle) · `tm-chotu-pii-register` (`drx_link`, prescription tables) · `tm-chotu-learnings` L21/L22 (the two mistakes this model recorded).
# frontend_engineer.md — Truemeds Doctor Portal Prototype

## Purpose

This file defines how Claude should behave while coding the Truemeds Doctor Portal prototype frontend.

This is a mobile-web consultation workflow prototype.
It is not a throwaway demo. Claude must not invent product logic, add screens outside the locked workflow, or rewrite the prototype freely.

---

## 1. Role

Claude is acting as a safe frontend engineer for this prototype.

Claude's job is to:
- make careful, targeted frontend changes
- preserve locked consultation workflow behavior
- build clean, readable, mobile-optimized code
- use mock data and simulated behavior where required
- avoid risky rewrites and unnecessary structural changes

---

## 2. Source of truth priority

Claude must follow this order:

1. `project_truth.md`
2. Other locked project rules
3. Existing code structure and working patterns
4. The current task request

If something is unclear, Claude must not invent product logic.

---

## 3. Non-negotiable guardrails

Claude must not:
- rewrite the whole prototype
- invent new workflow steps, states, or consultation logic
- make random UX or design changes unrelated to the task
- silently change locked CTA behavior or workflow gate behavior
- migrate to a framework or add heavy dependencies
- rename or delete files without approval
- modularize the single `index.html` without explicit instruction

Claude should keep changes:
- small
- targeted
- easy to review
- easy to reverse

---

## 4. V1 architecture rule

**[UPDATED 2026-07-13, `[USER-PROVIDED]`]**

V1 is now **three files**: `index.html` (structure), `styles.css` (all cosmetics/tokens/button system), `app.js` (all logic, including the `ICONS` map). This supersedes the original single-file rule — the user explicitly requested the split to fix CTA styling drift that kept happening inside one large file (same Schedule Callback button ended up with three different fonts/colors/icons across three call sites before the split).

Claude must not:
- split further than these three files (e.g. per-component files, per-section JS modules) without explicit instruction
- add a build step or bundler
- add an npm project or `package.json`
- add a framework (React, Vue, Svelte, etc.)
- reintroduce per-button/per-element bespoke cosmetic CSS — all button cosmetics go through the shared `.btn` system in `styles.css`; see `docs/design_system.md` before adding any new CTA

If further modularization is needed later, the user will ask explicitly.

---

## 5. File change rules

### 5.1 Default edit behavior
Work on one file or one small connected module at a time.
Before a multi-area edit within `index.html`, clearly state:
- which sections will change
- why each section needs to change
- the single purpose of the change

### 5.2 New file rule
Do not create new files without explicit approval.
V1 is `index.html` only.
The only exception is a `data/` folder for editable mock scenario JSON if the user asks for it.

---

## 6. Frontend architecture rules

Default direction for V1:
- Single `index.html` file
- Embedded CSS using CSS custom properties (design tokens as variables)
- Embedded vanilla JavaScript
- No external libraries beyond a CDN Tailwind link if needed
- No framework
- No build tooling

Code quality standards:
- separate visual sections with clear HTML comments
- keep JS functions focused and named clearly
- keep CSS organized by section (tokens, layout, components, states)
- avoid mixing consultation logic with display/render logic where practical

---

## 7. Mobile-first rule

**[LOCKED]**

This prototype is optimized for 360px–430px viewport width.

Claude should:
- use a single-column layout throughout
- optimize all touch targets for thumb reach on a phone
- avoid fixed sidebars, split panels, or horizontal scroll
- test all mock scenarios in a mobile viewport

Claude must not:
- design for desktop first and adapt down
- use tablet-style two-column layouts
- add a persistent side navigation

---

## 8. Single-scroll experience rule

**[LOCKED for V1]**

V1 is a single-scroll experience. No tabs. No nested pages.

All consultation workflow content — patient context, prescription, medicines, call actions, notes, final CTAs — should appear on one scrollable screen.

Sections that are not yet visible (e.g., post-call CTAs before valid call) should be hidden, not on a separate page.

Claude must not:
- add tab navigation
- add route-based page switching
- add a multi-step wizard structure

---

## 9. Mock data and scenario switching

The prototype supports five named scenarios from `project_truth.md` Section 7.

Claude should:
- implement scenario switching via a developer toggle (e.g., a visible debug bar at the top or a `?scenario=cat4` URL param)
- make each scenario produce a deterministic, complete screen state
- label all mock data clearly in code comments

Claude must not:
- make scenario behavior random
- hardcode a single scenario without a way to switch

---

## 10. Valid-call gate implementation rule

The valid-call gate is the most critical behavior in the prototype.

Implementation direction:
- Simulate a call timer in JS that counts from 0 when the "Call" button is pressed and the call is "connected"
- Post-call CTAs are hidden until the timer reaches 50 seconds
- At 50 seconds, reveal the correct CTA based on the active scenario's case type and HA status
- The timer is a mock simulation — exact timer behavior on hold/reconnect is `[OPEN DECISION]`

Claude must not:
- bypass the gate with a debug shortcut that is visible to a demo viewer
- show final CTAs before the timer reaches 50 seconds
- reveal the CTA on call initiation rather than call completion

---

## 11. State rule: one visible state object

Claude should maintain one main state object in JS — `DOCTOR_STATE`.

Purpose:
- track current consultation state (unassigned / assigned / calling / in-call / valid-call-completed / post-call)
- track active scenario
- track call timer
- avoid scattered globals

Claude should:
- update state in a clear, consistent way
- drive all UI visibility decisions from `DOCTOR_STATE`

Claude must not:
- scatter critical consultation state across unrelated DOM manipulations
- use random flags and counters without clear naming

---

## 12. ID and selector safety rule

All IDs must be unique and prefixed by section.

Examples:
- `call-btn-initiate`
- `call-timer-display`
- `cta-confirm-order`
- `cta-confirm-transfer`
- `medicines-list-section`
- `notes-input-field`

This prevents collisions in a single-page prototype.

---

## 13. Library rule

Do not add new libraries by default.

For V1, allowed dependencies via CDN only:
- Tailwind CSS (if used for styling)
- No other libraries

If a library is needed, Claude must explain:
- library name
- what problem it solves
- why native HTML/CSS/JS is not enough
- wait for approval

---

## 14. Scope control rule

Do not fix unrelated code by default.
If unrelated issues are noticed, mention them but do not silently expand scope.
One task = one task.

---

## 15. States and UI completeness rule

For meaningful sections, Claude should think beyond the happy path.

Where relevant, handle:
- loading / simulated delay states
- empty states (no prescription attached, no medicines, no notes yet)
- error states (call failed to connect, call dropped)
- disabled states (final CTAs disabled before valid call)

The prototype should feel like a real product shell, not a static screen.

---

## 16. Output / reporting rule

After each coding task, Claude should clearly state:
- what section of `index.html` changed
- what changed and why
- what was intentionally not changed
- assumptions made
- what is still mocked / fake / placeholder
- a short manual test plan

---

## 17. Manual testing rule

After a change, provide a short manual testing checklist:
- what to do
- what result should happen
- any edge case or mock scenario to check

Keep it simple and beginner-friendly.

---

## 18. Basic accessibility rule

At minimum:
- proper button semantics (`<button>` not `<div>` for tappable actions)
- labels for inputs where needed
- readable contrast on mobile
- visible disabled states for locked CTAs

Keep this practical and lightweight.

---

## 19. Final working principle

Claude should behave like a careful frontend engineer working inside a live mobile-first consultation workflow prototype.

That means:
- respect `project_truth.md`
- protect the valid-call gate and CTA routing behavior
- build in small safe steps
- keep code readable and easy to edit manually
- use mock behavior responsibly
- never take hidden product or architecture decisions on its own
# Truemeds Design System — Rules for building any UI

Read this before writing or changing any screen, prototype, mockup or page in this workspace.
It applies to every project: doctor portal, portal enhancements, internal tools, consumer flows.

**Source of truth: this folder.** It was built from SALT — the "TRUEMEDS Design Library (App)" Figma file from the design team (`figma.com/design/DcOIxZidim4Opva4DaDDRO`) — and extends it with Truemeds decisions listed in `beyond-salt.md`. When the design team sends an updated SALT file, merge it in deliberately (see README); it never overwrites anything in `beyond-salt.md` or `tokens/truemeds.json`. Changes to the design system are approved by Apurva.

---

## 1. How to use it

Link two files. Never copy values out of them.

```html
<link rel="stylesheet" href="../../design-system/truemeds.css">
<script src="../../design-system/icons/icons.js"></script>
<body class="tm"> …
```

(Adjust the relative path to your project's depth.)

- Colours, spacing, radius, shadows: `var(--tm-…)` semantic tokens only.
- Text: `.tm-text-*` classes (or the matching `--tm-font-size-*` / `--tm-font-lineheight-*` tokens).
- Components: `.tm-btn`, `.tm-field`, `.tm-chip`, `.tm-tag`, `.tm-card`, `.tm-row`, `.tm-notice`, `.tm-toast`, `.tm-sheet`, `.tm-appbar`, `.tm-actionbar`, `.tm-check`, `.tm-toggle`.
- Icons: `<span class="tm-icon" data-icon="phone"></span>`.
- See every token and component rendered in `preview.html`.

## 2. Hard rules

1. **No raw values.** No hex codes, no `rgb()`, no typed numbers for colour, spacing, radius, shadow, **sizes, border widths, timing, layering (z-index) or opacity** in component or project code (D-99). If a value isn't a token, it isn't allowed. Use a token by meaning, not by value (a 20px text line is `--tm-font-lineheight-sm`, not an icon size). The guard `python3 design-system/scripts/check_tokens.py` enforces this after every CSS edit and at every commit. Allowed exceptions carry a same-line `/* raw-ok: <reason> */` marker: geometry (`50%` centring, `100%`, `100vh`, `rotate(360deg)`, `line-height: 1`), optical nudges of 1–3px, the 31ch text measure, the hidden OTP input's 16px (stops iPhone zoom), reduced-motion `0.01ms`, `@media` widths, and prototype-only drawings (the drawn keyboard). Not scanned: `app/shell/`, `app/review/`, the mock Rx paper, `ui-screens/` reference files and the `[DEPRECATED]` legacy `.tm-otp` block.
2. **Semantic tokens only.** Use `--tm-content-*`, `--tm-surface-*`, `--tm-border-*`, `--tm-feedback-*`, `--tm-space-*`, `--tm-radius-*`. Never use `--tm-core-*` (the raw palette) in a project.
3. **One font:** Plus Jakarta Sans (comes with `truemeds.css`). No system-font stacks, no second typeface.
4. **One icon set:** Tabler outline via `icons.js`. Icons are monochrome and inherit text colour (default `--tm-content-secondary` or `--tm-content-primary`). **No emoji in UI.** No illustrated, multicolour or 3D icons (no shading, gradients or rendered depth; a one-colour line drawing of an object at an angle, like the `tablet` glyph, is fine). Glyphs Tabler lacks are drawn on its grid in `scripts/build-icons.mjs`, marked [PROPOSED]. Product images are real photos (`.tm-thumb`), never illustrations.
5. **Colour means state, never decoration.**
   - Brand blue (`--tm-content-brand`, `--tm-surface-brand-*`) = the main action and selection.
   - Green (`success`) = done / saved. Amber (`warning`) = needs attention. Red (`error`) = failed / destructive.
   - Everything else is neutral grey and white.
6. **One primary button per context.** Other actions use the button roles in §9.1: secondary → quiet (and destructive where something is removed or closed for good).
7. **Use what exists.** Need something new? Check `preview.html` and SALT first. If it truly isn't there, add it to `src/components.css` marked `[PROPOSED]` (built from tokens only) — never inside a project.
8. **Logo: LOCKED. Use the official files in `design-system/logo/`; never redraw, recolour, stretch, crop or hotlink them.** `truemeds-logo.svg` is the full logo (icon + wordmark, 110x26 viewBox, render at 130x31 in headers) and is the primary logo; Product-specific lockups (for example the Doctor app's "truemeds for doctors") are NOT design-system files: they live in their own project (`projects/<project>/brand/`), must embed `truemeds-logo.svg` unchanged, and carry their own lock check. `truemeds-icon.svg` is the icon-only mark (70x70, shape from the Truemeds-LOGO Figma file, https://www.figma.com/design/nD1pPa1SMzuZKnJIZxCdKa/Truemeds-LOGO) for favicons, avatars and tight spaces. **Colours are fixed: logo blue `#1B69DE` and logo green `#22B573`, in every logo file.** (The Figma file's older blue `#0071BC` is not used.) These are logo colours only; UI colour still comes from tokens (`--tm-content-brand` is blue-600 `#1D5DCF`, see `beyond-salt.md`). Reference with `<img src="…/design-system/logo/<file>.svg" alt="Truemeds">`. Lock: `logo/logo.lock.json` holds each file's sha256; `python3 design-system/scripts/check_logo.py` must pass before any commit that touches `logo/`. Changing a logo needs explicit approval from Apurva / the design team, then `check_logo.py --update`.

## 3. Choosing components

| Need | Use |
|---|---|
| The one main action | `.tm-btn.tm-btn--primary` (`--block` for full width on mobile) |
| Secondary action | `.tm-btn--secondary` (white, grey border, brand text, subtle shadow — as SALT) — §9.1 |
| Leave / cancel / dismiss | `.tm-btn--quiet` (grey text, no border) — §9.1. Tertiary, ghost and link-styled buttons are retired into Quiet. |
| Link inside a sentence | `.tm-btn--link` / plain link, brand colour |
| Destructive (remove or close for good) | `.tm-btn--destructive` (red border and text, never solid) — §9.1 |
| End a live call | `.tm-btn--call-end` (solid red) — only this use — §9.1 |
| Status label (Pending, Prescribed…) | `.tm-tag` + `--info / --success / --warning / --critical / --dark` |
| Filter or pick-one options | `.tm-chip` with `aria-pressed` |
| Text entry | `.tm-field` (label, control, helper; `--error / --success / --disabled`) |
| Grouped content | `.tm-card` (default shadow) or `--flat` / `--tint` |
| Item lists (medicines, orders) | `.tm-row` with `.tm-thumb` |
| Inline message | `.tm-notice` (`--warning`, `--error`) |
| Brief confirmation with nowhere else to show it | `.tm-toast` — dark, one line (two at most), above the bottom bar — §9.5 |
| Outcome of a task (e.g. case completed) | Status card — §9.3 |
| Mobile choice / detail panel | `.tm-sheet` over `.tm-overlay`, one template — §9.4 |
| A long wait that is not an action (calling, getting a result, checking) | `.tm-status-line` — plain text + spinner, no button shape (D-74). Busy button only for short waits right after a tap |
| A question above two answers | `.tm-notice--question` — plain centred text, no box (D-62) |
| Mobile sticky action | `.tm-actionbar` — white, 16px padding on all four sides (+ safe area), hairline top edge + upward shadow so it separates from white cards (D-62, D-67). **Every pinned main action sits in it** (Round 4, 2026-11-02): case page, Prescribe, Home, faster sign-in offer, returning unlock. The content above the bar scrolls. Sign-in and OTP keep their main button inline under the fields (§8.4), so they have no bar |
| A result notice inside a bar | `.tm-actionbar > .tm-notice` — edge-to-edge band on the bar's top edge, 1px bottom edge in the tone colour (§9.5, Round 4 item 3) |

Button sizes: `--lg` 56px for every full-width action button in a group (bar, sheet, form), default 48px for compact inline buttons. `--sm` 40px and `--xs` 32px are **not used for anything tappable on mobile** (below the 48px minimum, §8.6). Details in §9.1.

## 4. Mobile vs web / internal tools

SALT is built for the app. Use it as-is for mobile screens.

For **portals, desktop and internal tools**, use the same tokens and components plus:

- Web headings: `.tm-text-h1-web` (36/48), `.tm-text-h2-web` (32/40), `.tm-text-h3-web` (20/28) — these are in SALT.
- The **[PROPOSED] internal-tools layer** in `truemeds.css`: `.tm-page`, `.tm-grid`, `.tm-stack`, `.tm-sidenav`, `.tm-table`, `.tm-stat`. These are not in SALT yet; they are built from SALT tokens and pending design-team review.
- Internal tools are **denser and calmer** than the consumer app: body `md`/`sm` text, `.tm-card--flat` over shadows for dense layouts, tags instead of coloured backgrounds, no promotional elements (offer banners, savings strips, illustrations).

## 5. Design review checklist

Run before calling any UI task done. Every answer must be "yes".

- [ ] Only `truemeds.css` tokens/components — no hex, no raw values for colour/spacing/radius/shadow/size/border width/timing/layering/opacity (`check_tokens.py` passes)?
- [ ] Would the native app need a name for this value? If yes, it's a token.
- [ ] Plus Jakarta Sans only?
- [ ] All icons from `icons.js`, monochrome? Zero emoji?
- [ ] Colour used only for state (brand = action/selection, green/amber/red = status)?
- [ ] Exactly one primary button visible per context?
- [ ] Status shown with `.tm-tag`, not custom pills?
- [ ] No cards nested inside cards?
- [ ] Anything new marked `[PROPOSED]` in the design system, not invented in the project?
- [ ] Checked against `preview.html` side by side?
- [ ] Four text levels only, from §8.1?
- [ ] One side margin and only the three gaps from §8.3?
- [ ] Main button placed per §8.4 (inline on typing screens, pinned otherwise)?
- [ ] Small phone (360×640): nothing the doctor needs is hidden without a scroll cue, and the pinned bar never covers the last content (§8.14)?
- [ ] All text passes AA contrast on its actual background (§8.7)?
- [ ] Buttons use the four roles and one height per group (§9.1)? Every tap target at least 48px (§8.6)?
- [ ] Chips wrap instead of squeezing; grids use one chip size per screen (§9.2)?
- [ ] Every sheet follows the one template (§9.4)? Toasts only where nothing on screen can show the result (§9.5)?
- [ ] Every state in §9.6 that applies is designed and rendered (loading, empty, failed, offline, busy, outcome, disabled, read-only, long content)?
- [ ] Sentence case, formats and accessibility per §9.7–9.8?

## 6. Known gaps (open with design team)

- SALT has two generations of variables. This folder uses the new set (`Colour/…`): brand surfaces blue-500 `#266CE1`, brand **text** blue-600 `#1D5DCF` (Truemeds override for AA, `beyond-salt.md`). Some SALT components still reference the older `tm_semantic_*` set (brand `#1B69DE`, which truemeds.in also uses). Design team is aware; a side-by-side check against the Figma library is pending — do not claim full SALT parity until then.
- SALT's Font page lists "SF Pro Display" as the typeface; variables, components and truemeds.in all use Plus Jakarta Sans. We use Plus Jakarta Sans.
- "Heading XL" on the Font page is 32px on a 28px line height — looks like a typo, so it is left out.
- No web/desktop library yet; the internal-tools layer above fills the gap until one exists.

**Backlog — not in the system yet (2026-10-15):**
- **Data table behaviour** for portals: sorting, filters, pagination, row selection and actions, loading / empty / error, long values, narrow layouts, keyboard and screen-reader behaviour.
- **Workflow patterns** (prescription review, substitute comparison, payment retry, refund timeline, hand-off / escalation): built first in the product project that needs them; moved here only when a second product needs the same pattern.
- **Deprecated classes** `.tm-btn--tertiary`, `.tm-btn--ghost` (aliases of quiet): remove once every project has migrated to the §9.1 roles.

## 7. Phone-frame prototypes (layout rule — do not re-litigate)

Mobile-first prototypes are previewed on desktop as a **phone frame at the real phone shape (390 × 844, about 19.5:9)**, never as a stretched page, a narrow column glued to the window, or a dialog layout. *(Changed 2026-11-09, Apurva, doctor portal D-97: was 9:16, which is wider and shorter than today's phones and than the approved pictures.)*

1. **Below 768px** the app *is* the screen: the window scrolls and overlays are viewport-fixed.
2. **At 768px and up** the app sits in a centred phone frame at 390 : 844: `height: clamp(640px, 100vh - 48px, 844px)`, `width: height × 390/844`, so it is exactly 390 × 844 when the window allows and scales down evenly when it doesn't. Rounded border, `overflow: hidden`, `transform: translateZ(0)`.
3. **One scroll area** inside the frame (the content region). The header is pinned above it. The page behind never scrolls because of the frame, and no side panel may be a scroll container.
4. **Overlays live inside the frame.** Bottom sheets, full-screen viewers, toasts and snackbars are DOM children of the frame (the transform makes `position: fixed` anchor to it). Bottom sheets keep the mobile bottom-sheet behaviour at every width; they are never turned into centred dialogs on desktop.
5. **Demo/dev panels** float beside the frame (absolutely positioned), **top-aligned with the frame's top edge**, in **one column on the right** of the frame. They never change its size or centring.
6. Desktop overrides go in one block at the END of the stylesheet so they beat the mobile rules.
7. Check before every commit that touches layout: `python3 projects/<project>/docs/layout_check.py` must say ALL PASS (frame 390 : 844, panels top-aligned, wheel scroll works, sheet at frame bottom, Rx viewer fills frame, no page errors).

## 8. Mobile screen layout  [ADDED 2026-10-14 · layout v2 · D-42–D-45]

1. **Type scale — four levels per screen.**

   | Level | Use | Style |
   |---|---|---|
   | L1 | Screen title | `.tm-text-title-lg` 32/40 semibold |
   | L2 | Key content (values, names, the thing acted on) | `.tm-text-body-lg` 16/24 (or `-strong` where emphasis matters) |
   | L3 | Supporting text (descriptions, helper, status lines) | `.tm-text-body-md` 14/20, `--tm-content-secondary` |
   | L4 | Fine print (consent, legal) | `.tm-text-body-sm` 12/16, `--tm-content-secondary` |

   Only the screen title is large; buttons keep their design-system style. Don't introduce in-between sizes.
   **Small sizes:** 9–11px text tokens are never used in apps. 12px only for tags and L4 fine print. Anything the user needs to act on (clinical details, amounts, status, errors) is 14px or larger.
   The type scale applies to screen text. Component internals (OTP digits, button labels, keyboard) keep their component specs.
2. **Side margin:** `--tm-space-page` (**16**, the design team's standard — changed from 24 on 2026-10-26, D-67) on every mobile screen, including the header, sheets and the action bar. Every block — fields, notices, cards, code boxes, buttons — shares the same left and right edges.
3. **Three gaps only:** tight `--tm-space-sm` (8): label↔field, field↔helper, and a result notice ↔ the button it came from (A-14). **A result notice under a field sits 16 below the field** (the field's 8 gap plus the notice's 8; D-98) · related `--tm-space-lg` (16): items within a group, **and cards stacked one above another — the same gap everywhere cards stack, on the case page, Prescribe and Profile (B-04, B-05, A-13, 2026-11-02)** · section `--tm-space-2xl` (32): between groups. Spare vertical space goes between the content and a pinned button, never inside a group.
   Home-base screens use 3xl (48) as the hero gap between logo and title.
4. **Main button placement:**
   - **Screens with text entry:** the primary button sits inline directly under the content, so it stays visible above the keyboard without moving.
   - **Screens without text entry:** the primary button is pinned to the bottom with the safe-area inset; a secondary/quiet action sits below it.
   - **Pinned bar (Round 4, 2026-11-02):** every pinned main action — case page, Prescribe, Home, faster sign-in offer, returning unlock — sits in `.tm-actionbar` (§3). The content above it scrolls; the bar never covers the last content at the end of the scroll (§8.14). Toasts sit above the bar.
5. **Header types** (each product decides which screens use which):
   - **Home-base header:** product logo/lockup left, icon actions right.
   - **Task header:** Back + screen title, no logo. **Back only where there is a previous screen** to return to; screens that open a journey (e.g. a launch screen, a post-sign-in offer) have none. Two sizes:
     - **Hero** — tall tinted band, title `.tm-text-title-lg` 32/40. For short step screens with little content.
     - **Compact** — short sticky tinted band, title `.tm-text-h1` 20/28, meta lines under it. For dense work screens. Never changes height while scrolling. When Back is hidden, the title and meta move to the left margin. Long titles wrap to at most two lines; the height is set per screen, never while scrolling.  [ENHANCED 2026-10-15]
   - **Edit screen header** [ADDED 2026-10-15] — for a full-screen editor opened over a page (e.g. Prescribe). White bar: ✕ on the left (closes; asks before discarding unsaved changes), title 20/28 with an optional one-line subtitle, at most one action on the right (destructive outline if it removes something). A pinned summary band may sit directly under it.
   - **Viewer header** [ADDED 2026-10-15] — for full-screen viewers (e.g. a prescription image). Dark bar: ✕, title, tools (zoom, rotate, fit) using `.tm-btn--on-dark`. The title never overlaps the tools; it truncates first.
   - **Header edge (B-07, Round 5, Apurva 2026-11-02; Round 6 reading):** in the compact task header, the edit header and the viewer header, the Back / ✕ button is a 48px target whose 20px glyph starts on the 16px page margin (the target overhangs 14px to the left; glyph centre 26px from the edge), and the title starts 54px from the edge on every header type alike. The sheet ✕ uses the same edge (§9.4).
6. **Tap targets:** **48px minimum** for everything tappable that is not a link inside a sentence: icon buttons (✕, ?, Back, zoom), buttons, chips, rows, toggles, standalone links. Enlarge the hit area, not the text. Meets Android (48dp) and iOS (44pt); WCAG 2.5.8's floor is 24px. Links inside a sentence follow WCAG 2.5.8's inline exception.  [CHANGED 2026-10-15, was 44]
7. **Contrast:** all text meets WCAG AA (4.5:1; 3:1 for 24px+ or 18.66px+ bold) on the surface it sits on, including tinted bands and notices. Blue means "tap me" (§2.5): links are always `--tm-content-brand`, never grey-and-underlined. Disabled controls are exempt from the contrast rule (WCAG 1.4.3) and keep the design-system disabled style.
8. **Loading primary:** brand-blue fill, white label and white spinner (`.tm-btn--busy` with `.tm-spinner`) on every screen. Never grey, pale or disabled-looking.
9. **Keyboard clearance:** keep at least 16px between the inline main button and an open keyboard. If the content does not fit, the content scrolls.
10. **Titles:** no full stop at the end of a screen title. Titles wrap balanced (L8).
11. **Decorative icons** (bullets, list markers) are neutral (`--tm-content-secondary`); brand blue is for actions and selection only (§2.5). An icon tile used as a screen's hero may stay brand-tinted.
12. **Value display:** a large status value (e.g. time remaining) is a component, not screen text: 32/40 semibold, fixed-width digits (L9). Like OTP digits, it sits outside the four-level type scale.
13. **Logo lockup size and place (A-07, 2026-11-02).** Every home-base screen with the "truemeds for doctors" lockup (`brand/truemeds-for-doctors.svg`, project level, §2.8) draws it **166px wide** (height follows the 220×54 artwork), **16px from the top**, and the logo→title gap stays as in §8.3 (L6). 166 is the largest width that fits Home at 360 wide with 16 to the first header icon: 360 − 2×16 − (3 × 48 + 2 × 8 − 14 for the actions' 14px overhang) − 16 = 166. Nothing else sets the lockup size.
14. **Small phones (Round 4, 2026-11-02).** At **360 × 640** every state of a screen is checked: nothing the doctor needs is hidden without a scroll cue (a visible edge of the next item, or a scroll area), and a pinned bar never covers the last content when the content is scrolled to its end. The check runs as a permanent guard in `ui-screens/feedback/check-locked-specs.py` (small-phone pass, every state, Round 4 item 10; `docs/layout_check.py` covers the old prototype only). Prescribe, the case page, Home, the offer and the unlock must pass it before a screen is approved.

### Locked specs (D-46, D-47) — set once in the design system, never per screen

These are fixed. A change needs Apurva's approval and an update to D-46. `ui-screens/feedback/check-locked-specs.py` fails the build if any of them is violated.

| ID | Spec |
|---|---|
| L1 | OTP code boxes (`.tm-otp-slots`): square 56×56 slots, 24px digits, one tight centred group, gap 8 (`--tm-space-sm`). Never stretched, never 16px digits. |
| L2 | Loading primary buttons: brand-blue fill, white label, white spinner, on every screen (sign-in "Sending OTP", OTP "Verifying", any future loading primary). |
| L3 | OTP keyboard stays open during "Verifying" (D-44). Only locked and expired-and-resend-limited close it. |
| L4 | Field helper and field error text: 14/20 (`.tm-field__helper`). |
| L5 | Notice text (`.tm-notice`): 14/20. |
| L6 | Sign-in logo-to-title gap: 48 (`--tm-space-3xl`), home-base hero gap. |
| L7 | Field helper/error text aligns with its field: left under full-width fields, centred under the centred OTP code boxes (D-47). |
| L8 | Screen titles wrap balanced — never a single word alone on the last line (D-47). |
| L9 | Value display (e.g. Home time remaining): 32/40 semibold, fixed-width digits (D-47). |

## 9. Components and states  [ADDED 2026-10-15 · design-system review · checked against Material 3, Apple HIG, WCAG 2.2]

Set once in the design system; a project never restyles them. Where `src/components.css` does not yet match a rule below, the rule wins and the component is updated centrally (tracked in `beyond-salt.md`).

### 9.1 Buttons
| Role | Look | Use for |
|---|---|---|
| **Primary** | Solid brand blue, white label | The one action that moves the flow forward. One per screen or sheet. |
| **Secondary** | White, grey border (`--tm-border-default`), brand label, subtle shadow (`--tm-shadow-button-secondary`) — as SALT | A real alternative action, less likely than the primary. |
| **Quiet** | Grey text, no border | Leave, cancel, dismiss, "not now", "back to …". |
| **Destructive** | White, red border and label — never solid | Removes or closes something for good (disable, mark unavailable). Never the primary role (Apple HIG). |
| **Call end** (special) | Solid red, white label | Only for ending a live call (the phone convention). Nothing else uses it. |

- **One height per group** — differ by style, not size (Apple HIG). Full-width action buttons in a pinned bar, sheet or form: **56px, 18 semibold**, whatever their role. Compact inline buttons (e.g. "View Rx" in a content row): **48px, 16 semibold**.
- **Icons on buttons (2026-10-24):** primary and secondary buttons carry an icon only when it helps the user recognise the action at a glance — calling, scheduling, viewing, skipping, direction (→ next). No icon when the label must be read: confirming, finalising, releasing, and answers to a question. Quiet buttons never have icons. One icon per button, from `icons.js`, before the label (direction arrows after). **Scope:** labelled buttons only — header icons, the close ×, status and notice icons are not buttons and are not covered; adding time ("Add time" with a plus) counts as scheduling.
- **Order, top to bottom:** primary → secondary → quiet. Two secondaries may sit side by side (equal width, label fits on one line at 360px wide with room to spare; otherwise shorten the label or stack). (D-54)
- **Icons in buttons:** only when they add meaning (e.g. a phone on "Call patient"). On a side-by-side pair, **both buttons or neither** carry an icon (D-54).
- **Labels:** verb first, sentence case, no full stop, never "Click" / "Tap".
- **States:** pressed; disabled (design-system disabled style, exempt from contrast); busy (L2). Busy spinner → label gap is 8 on every busy button (A-08, 2026-11-02). A quiet action in progress shows text only ("Ending…"), no spinner. **Busy labels always end with "…"** ("Verifying…", "Sending OTP…") (D-54).
- Links inside a sentence stay links (brand colour), not buttons.
- **Button icon size steps with the button size** (Apurva, 2026-11-02; replaces the D-81 F27 pair-only rule): **56px action buttons (18px label) → 20px icon; 48px compact buttons (16px label) → 16px icon.** Icons stay on the icon scale (16 / 20 / 24) — like type and spacing, each size is a step on a fixed scale, never an in-between value; the icon is one step at or just above its label (as in Carbon, Atlassian and Fluent). **Icon-only buttons** (Back, Help, notifications, Profile, close ×, viewer controls) are not labelled buttons and keep a **20px** icon. Set centrally in `components.css` by button size (`.tm-btn--lg` → 20, compact → 16, `.tm-btn--icon` → 20), overriding any size in the markup; screens never size button icons. SALT's code copy states no per-button icon size; this is a Truemeds addition (`beyond-salt.md`).

### 9.2 Chips and choice grids
- **Free-flowing chips** (e.g. frequency, food timing, date): fixed padding and font, width hugs the label, **wrap to the next row** when they don't fit. Never squeeze, clip or shrink text.
- **Grid chips** (e.g. dose per time of day, extra doses, time slots): equal-width columns on a 4-column grid. **Every grid on one screen uses the same chip size**; a row with fewer options leaves cells empty (left-aligned) instead of stretching. **A grid starts at its label's left edge**; only a grid with row labels (M / A / N) sits after the label column (D-54). **One chip size per screen:** grids without row labels use the same column width as the M / A / N grid and leave the extra space on the right. Grid chips have 8px side padding — the column sets the size. **Words are free chips, numbers are grid chips:** units (Days / Weeks / Months / Ongoing), frequencies and food timing wrap as free chips; doses, counts and time slots sit in grids (D-54).
- Selected = brand tint + brand border (`aria-pressed`). 48px minimum height.

### 9.3 Lists, tags and cards
- **Rows:** thumb · title · meta · trailing. A **chevron** only when the row opens something. **Row with a status:** title on line 1 (full width); line 2 = one short fact on the left (14/20 semibold, e.g. "Qty 30") and the status tag on the right (`.tm-row__line`, D-54). Details that belong to an edit screen (dose, duration) are not repeated in the row. **Read-only rows** (e.g. a closed task): muted text, no chevron, not tappable, still readable.
- **Status tags:** colour by meaning — done = success, needs action = warning, removed / inactive = neutral, failed = critical. **Reserve the tag width** so the row text never re-wraps when the status changes. Tag text meets AA.
- **Cards:** one level only, never a card inside a card.
- **Status card** (outcome of a task): icon + title (optional one line under it), stays on screen. Not a toast. **Tinted by outcome** (decided D-62, 2026-10-25): `--success` (confirmed, transferred, forwarded — success tint, tick), `--neutral` (pending or no outcome — grey tint, clock / phone-off / info icon), `--warning` (completed with a problem the user must act on — amber tint, warning icon). A plain white status card is not used: the outcome must not look like another content card. **Edge, not shadow** (2026-10-25, option C5): every tone has a 1px edge in its colour (success green-300, neutral grey-200, warning amber-700) and no drop shadow, so it reads as a result, not as a tappable card. Neutral keeps the grey-100 fill.
- **Empty state:** `.tm-empty`.

### 9.4 Bottom sheets — one template
| Part | Rule |
|---|---|
| Background | Page dimmed behind the sheet (`.tm-overlay`). |
| Top | Rounded top corners; **no drag handle** (the ✕ is always there). |
| Header | Title 20/28 semibold, left · ✕ right (48px target, glyph on the 16 margin — the same as the compact task header, B-07) · **no divider**. Optional one-line subtitle (L3). |
| Spacing | 16px side margins (`--tm-space-page`, same as screens) · 16px header → content · 24px + safe area at the bottom. |
| Content | Fields, chips, or **option rows** (one shared style for reason lists: title + one-line description, full width, 48px+; tapping applies). Labels inside a sheet ("Select reason", "Date", "Time") use `.tm-sheet__label` — one style, 8 above the field (B-14, 2026-11-02). |
| Actions | §9.1 order and size (56px, full width). **Pinned (Round 6, 2026-11-02):** the actions are the sheet's last part and stay on screen when the content is taller than the space — the content scrolls between the pinned header and the pinned actions. The hairline and soft upward shadow show only while content scrolls under them (`design-system/src/sheet-scroll.js` sets `.is-stuck`; screens with sheets load it). Sheet buttons stay fully visible and tappable at 390×844 and 360×640. Reason lists need no button. **Confirmation sheet** ("Leave this case?", "Discard changes?"): the action the sheet asks about on top — red outline if it can't easily be undone, primary otherwise — and the "stay" option as quiet text below. A sheet offering two real actions (e.g. Retry call / Mark as unavailable) is a choice, not a confirmation (D-54). |
| Closing | ✕, tap outside or swipe down = cancel — **except while an action is in progress**. Unsaved input asks before closing. |
| Size | Up to 85% of the screen height; the content scrolls, header and actions stay pinned. Max width 640px on desktop. |
| Over the bar | **A sheet always covers the pinned action bar of the screen behind it (Round 6, 2026-11-02).** The scrim and the sheet sit above the bar in every state: no part of the bar shows or takes a tap while a sheet is open. |
- **Quiet text for "stay" (D-81 F11):** the option that keeps things as they are ("Stay signed in", "Keep available") is quiet text (`tm-btn--quiet`), never a secondary button.

### 9.5 Feedback
| Surface | Rule |
|---|---|
| **Field error** | Under the field (L4, L7). |
| **Notice** | Neutral / warning / error (L5); stays until the cause is gone. **Warning** when something blocks the main action or runs on a timer (offline, locks, near expiry); **error** when an action failed; **neutral** only for side information (D-54). Text wraps balanced (A-22). **Placement (A-19, Round 4 item 2, 2026-11-02):** a notice that is the **result of an action** sits next to the control that did it — on a screen with a pinned bar, inside the bar above its button, in the band style of §3 (Home "ending soon", setup / add / request failures, the offer's "Couldn't turn on…", the case and calling bar notices); on a screen without a bar, 16 below the field it came from (D-98) or 8 below the button (A-14). A notice about **the bar's own action** — the result of the last tap, or why its button is disabled now (offline, "No cases right now") — is also in the bar. A notice about the **screen's general situation** (unlock "isn't available", Home idle-expired, manually ended, expired-pending, stop-pending) stays at the top of the panel, not tied to a button (lane-request 7, Round 4). **Alignment (D-86, 2026-11-03):** the band inside the bar is centred (icon and text as a group, wrapped lines centred, balanced breaks kept); every other notice (top of page, inside sheets, in content) stays left-aligned. |
| **Status card** | The outcome of a task (§9.3). |
| **Toast** | **Dark** (inverse surface — the platform standard), **14/20**, 12px × 16px padding, medium radius, one line (two at most), width up to the screen minus margins. Just above the bottom bar (on a screen with a pinned bar, `--tm-toast-offset` = bar height + 16, Round 4), **one at a time**, about 4 seconds, polite screen-reader announcement, never takes focus. **Only when the result has no place on the screen**; otherwise show it where it happens. |
| **Loading** | Busy primary (L2). |
| **Rule** | One message per cause — never field error + notice + toast for the same thing. |

**Keyboard (D-81):** when a text field is focused, the field scrolls above the keyboard with its label and helper visible (so the doctor can see what they type into). The pinned action bar hides while the keyboard is open and returns when it closes (same reason as §8 point 9, 16px clearance). Applies to Prescribe and the case page.

### 9.6 States every screen must cover
A screen is not done until each state that applies is designed and rendered: **loading · empty · error / failed · offline · busy (in progress) · success / outcome · disabled · read-only · long content** (long names, two-line wraps at 360px wide).

### 9.7 Content and formats
- Sentence case for buttons, titles, labels and sheet titles. No full stop on titles; full stops on sentences in notices and helpers.
- Time **3:00 PM** · date **12 Aug 2024** · money **₹1,240** · timers **m:ss** (9:42).
- Say what happened and what to do next. No blame, no jargon.

### 9.8 Accessibility
- Contrast AA (§8.7); tap targets 48px (§8.6).
- **Visible focus ring** on buttons, links and tappable rows. Text fields and the OTP code show focus with their own border (no outline on top); a sheet or screen focused by script shows no outline (D-54).
- **Accessible name** on every icon-only button (Back, Help, Close, Zoom in).
- Toasts and status changes announced politely; never steal focus.
- Animations (sheets, toasts) respect the phone's reduce-motion setting.

### 9.9 Out of scope unless a project opts in
Dark mode · large text / dynamic type (tested in the native app) · haptics and animation detail (native) · desktop layouts beyond §7.

## 10. How screens are designed and reviewed (all projects)  [ADDED 2026-10-15]

The screen version of the workspace rule "Definition of done" (`AGENTS.md`). Lesson: `knowledge/learnings/2026-10-15-define-done-before-work.md`.

1. **Standards before screens.** Every component and state a screen set needs exists here (§9) before anything is rendered. Missing → add it centrally first, never inside the project.
2. **Checklist before review.** Each project starts from `design-system/templates/standards-checklist.md`, adds its own decisions, and gets Apurva's OK. Claude, Codex and Apurva all review against it, not taste.
3. **Automatic checks** run on every captured state at 360, 390 and 430 wide; a state with a failing check is not shown for review.
4. **Concepts come from the real source** — the real screen with only the agreed changes. No invented sections, content or buttons.
5. **Projects never restyle central components**; old shared stylesheets are not loaded by concepts.
6. **Shared fixes first**, then screens; every round is compared with the last approved finals.
7. **Decisions recorded before building** (project decision log), then the build prompt.
8. **Reviewer feedback is vetted**: adopt what's valid, log what's out of scope, flag doubts.
9. **Every state planned in the coverage list** before rendering (§9.6), including stress content.
10. **Finish line:** checks pass, every checklist line passes, reviewers report no blockers, Apurva approves. Then frozen: new findings are a bug (fix where it occurs) or a next-version idea (logged).


